- 009 first draft "— ini bukan jaminan hasil." tripped the ms-cost-guarantee rule; reworded to
  "— ini bukan janji tentang hasil."
- 013 first draft "perundingan dalam bahasa Melayu tidak ditawarkan" tripped ms-consult-lang (the regex has no
  negation awareness); reworded to the positive limiter.

## 5. Disagreements with the reviews (not applied)
1. b#9 (012) — "Di Taiwan kenderaan di kanan (berbeza dengan Malaysia)". Inserting a statement about
   Malaysian traffic law is exactly what R2/ACCEPTED-ms forbid (no claims about the reader's country) and it
   breaks the nationality gate. Only the decimal format was fixed. If the supervisor wants the RHT fact, it
   should be added as a Taiwan-side statement ("Di Taiwan, kenderaan memandu di sebelah kanan jalan") with no
   comparison — say the word and I will add it.
2. b#65 (018) — the reviewer's replacement sentence ("rangka umum laluan pendaftaran") describes a different
   thing than the English source sentence, which really is about contract design. Applied a minimal
   disambiguation instead of the suggested rewrite.
3. b#34 / "pelupusan" — the reviewer calls 'pelupusan' a raw calque. It is only wrong in the one
   mettre-à-la-disposition sentence (fixed). 'Pelupusan harta' is standard Malaysian legal usage for disposal
   of property (007, 016) and was deliberately left.
4. a#43 "sekali gus" — "sekali gus" means "all at once/simultaneously", which is not what 停業 1 year per
   registration says. Rephrased for sense rather than using the suggested word.
5. a#3 — the reviewer's suggested gloss mentioned Malaysian termination benefits ("pampasan buang kerja di
   Malaysia"); that is a claim about Malaysian law, so the neutral ACCEPTED-ms form ('skim atau peraturan
   negara lain') was used.
6. b#59 / a#48 "ganti rugi bukan wang" vs "pampasan penderitaan": both were suggested. 慰撫金 is used
   consistently as 'pampasan penderitaan' and 非財產上損害 as 'ganti rugi bukan wang', so the two Chinese terms
   stay distinguishable in Malay.

